<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Overview</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Keystone Scheduler is a planning tool for Keystone Academy, a school in Beijing. It helps
    administrators assign teachers, sections and rooms across an 8-day rotating timetable, compare
    “what-if” scenarios, and keep the final say over the result.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Astha IT in Dhaka built it for the school. I was the UI/UX designer, the only designer on a team
    that also covered project management, business analysis, AI, frontend and backend development,
    QA and DevOps. I designed the screens from sign-in through to role permissions.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Names, emails and figures in the screens below are placeholder data.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">The problem</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Keystone runs an 8-day rotating schedule. Every cycle has to line up teachers, student groups,
    classrooms and time slots, and the timetable was built by hand in spreadsheets. That caused four
    problems:
  </p>
  <ul class="cs__p">
    <li><strong>Workload mismatches.</strong> Teachers ended up over or under their maximum load.</li>
    <li><strong>Idle or overfull rooms.</strong> Venues were not matched to what each course needed.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inconsistent assignments.</strong> Subjects were not always taught by qualified staff.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Little transparency.</strong> Staff could not see how decisions were made.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="cs__p">
    As the school grows, so does the work. Administrators, teachers and planners would all use the
    new tool, so I framed the design around one question: how can an optimizer’s output stay
    understandable and adjustable, so an administrator can see why a schedule looks the way it does,
    change the rules, and try alternatives without losing earlier work?
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">How the work ran</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The plan was 15 weeks of two-week sprints: scope and wireframes first, then the constraint panel
    and schedule visualizer, then scenarios and role-based access, and finally testing, deployment
    and staff training. Each design went to Keystone for approval, with up to two rounds of feedback
    per wireframe.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    In Figma, the design moved through a moodboard, ideation, an audit and a UI review, then colour
    variations, several trial-and-error UI rounds and different versions of the authentication flow,
    before a presentation for development and a final round of UI feedback.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Four design decisions</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Each decision answers a requirement from the project brief.
  </p>
  <h3 class="cs__h3">1. Put the rules next to the result</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The brief asked for a rule engine where each constraint can be hard or soft, with preference
    weights. I put a Settings panel beside the staffing grid. Each constraint has an on/off toggle,
    a weight out of 10 and a Hard Constraint flag, and a set of rules can be saved and switched
    between Current and Saved. Changing a rule and seeing its effect happen in one view.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-constraints.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1324"
      height="828"
      alt="Schedule and Constraint screen: five KPI tiles across the top, a Settings panel of constraints with toggles, weights and Hard Constraint checkboxes on the left, and the staffing grid on the right. Teacher rows are blurred."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The constraint editor beside the staffing grid. Teacher rows are blurred for privacy.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3 class="cs__h3">2. Show a schedule’s health at a glance</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The brief asked for real-time feedback through summary metrics. Five tiles sit above the grid:
    Allocation Success, Teacher Balance Index, Soft Constraint Violations, Resource Utilization and
    Unscheduled Items. Good and bad values use green and red, and the last tile links to the
    unscheduled list. The dashboard carries the same idea further, with a ranked overview of every
    scenario, so an administrator can judge a run before reading a single row.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-dashboard.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="905"
      alt="Dashboard with four colour-coded KPI tiles, a Scenario Overview table with progress bars, violation counts and rank badges, and four quick action cards."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The dashboard: KPI tiles, a ranked scenario overview and quick actions such as Run
      Optimization.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3 class="cs__h3">3. Make experimenting safe</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The brief asked for scenarios saved with timestamps that can be reloaded, compared or continued.
    Each saved scenario is a card with its saved time, status, version, rank and key metrics.
    Compare stays disabled until two scenarios are selected, and a hint says so. A detail view lists
    the unscheduled items and the version history. Trying an alternative never costs earlier work.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-scenarios.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1324"
      height="828"
      alt="Scenario Management page with six scenario cards showing saved time, status, version, overall rank and metrics, each with Add to Compare and View Details. The Compare button is disabled, with the hint: Select at least two scenarios to enable comparison."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Scenario cards. Compare stays disabled until two are selected.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-detail.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1514"
      height="946"
      alt="Scenario detail with metrics at the top, a table of unscheduled courses with colour-graded success-rate badges, and a Version History panel on the right with its own Compare action."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Scenario detail: unscheduled items with success-rate badges, and version history alongside.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3 class="cs__h3">4. Control who can do what</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The brief asked for role-based access for administrators, teachers and planners. I designed a
    permission matrix of six modules (Teacher, Course, Venue, Schedule, Scenario and User
    Management) against Add, Edit, View and Delete, plus user-group screens for adding users and
    roles. Every permission for a role is readable in a single table.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-roles.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="875"
      alt="Add Role page with a role title field and a permission matrix of modules against Add, Edit, View and Delete checkboxes."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Roles and permissions in one matrix.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Keeping a dense grid workable</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The staffing grid carries teacher, department, load, preps and per-section counts across dozens
    of columns. To keep it usable, I added department filter chips, a zoom control, and separate
    Staffing Grid and Schedule tabs, so planners can narrow the view instead of scrolling through
    everything.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Getting in and setting up</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Sign-in is a single focused card with the school crest. A tilted, blurred timetable on the left
    shows what is behind the login. The full flow also covers verification, a four-step
    forgot-password path, error states, account creation and Microsoft sign-in.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-signin.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="905"
      alt="Sign-in screen with the Keystone Academy crest, email and password fields and a Sign In button, beside a royal blue panel with a tilted, blurred timetable."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Sign-in, with a glimpse of the timetable behind it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Teacher management is a searchable list showing each teacher’s teaching load, home room and
    grade, with view, edit and delete on every row. Schools can add teachers in bulk through Upload
    CSV.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-teachers.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1514"
      height="946"
      alt="Teacher Management list with search, Add Teacher, Upload CSV and Export actions, and a table of teachers with email, teaching load, home room and grade."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Teacher management, with bulk upload by CSV.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Visual system</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    A calm blue shell keeps colour for meaning. The header uses a royal blue (#2547D0) and actions a
    brighter blue (#335CFF), while the KPI tiles use blue, green, purple and teal. Scenarios are
    graded from Excellent to Poor, and the same scale appears on rank badges and success-rate pills.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Every screen shares one top navigation, the same page-header pattern and the same pagination, so
    each new page feels familiar.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">What the project set out to achieve</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">These were the targets in the project brief, not measured results:</p>
  <ul class="cs__p">
    <li>Cut scheduling time from weeks to minutes.</li>
    <li>Complete an optimization run within 3 minutes on recommended hardware.</li>
    <li>Give repeatable results for unchanged inputs.</li>
    <li>Let administrators lock assignments and re-optimize the rest.</li>
    <li>Keep data on the school’s own infrastructure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Planned next steps included conflict heatmaps to show bottlenecks, AI suggestions for relaxing
    constraints when a schedule cannot be solved, and a teacher feedback module before schedules are
    finalised.
  </p>
</section>
